title: LSP Completion - (Example)

\procedure lsp.cp.pair(a, b:"B") <<a>> <<b>>

Open this tiddler's `.tid` file in your editor. On a new line at the end, type each entry below up to its end, and press ctrl-space if no list appears by itself. Nothing needs saving: the server answers from the text in the editor.

|! Type this |! You should get |
|`[[LS` |every title starting with ''LSP'', this one included |
|`[[l` |titles starting with ''l'' first, then titles with a later word starting with it |
|`[[ed` |''LSP Editor Setup'', because `ed` starts the word ''Editor'' |
|`[[dit` |nothing: `dit` sits inside ''Editor'' but starts no word |
|`[[LS S` |''LSP Editor Setup'' and ''LSP Server'': `LS` starts one word, `S` another |
|`[[lsp ed` |''LSP Editor Setup'' alone |
|`[[$:/core/ui/Page` |shadow tiddlers such as `$:/core/ui/PageTemplate`, which a plain prefix never reaches |
|`<<lsp.cp` |`lsp.cp.pair`, defined at the top of this tiddler |
|`<<lsp.cp.pair a:"1" ` |`b:`, the one parameter not given |
|`<$transclude $variable="lsp.cp.pair" ` |`a=` and `b=` |
|`<$li` |`list`, and every other widget starting with `li` |
|`<$link to="LSP Ser` |''LSP Server'': `to` takes a title |
|`<$link tooltip="LSP` |nothing: `tooltip` is text, not a title |
|`{{{ [ta` |`tag`, `tagging`, `tags` and the other operators starting with `ta` |
|`{{{ [<lsp` |`lsp.cp.pair` |
|`{{{ [tag[lsp` |''LSP'' and every other tag starting with `lsp`, each with how many tiddlers carry it |
|`{{{ [has[cap` |`caption` and every other field name starting with `cap` |
|`{{{ [field:mod` |`modified`: the suffix of `field:` names a field too |
|`{{{ [prefix[lsp` |nothing: a literal operand is no name |

Results were checked against this wiki. They change as you add tiddlers.

